Java Array Basics

Declaring and creating arrays

java
int[] nums = new int[5];        // creates array of 5 ints, all default to 0
int[] nums2 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};  // declare + initialize directly
int[] nums3 = new int[]{1, 2, 3};

In Java, arrays have a fixed size once created — you can't grow or shrink them. That's why "remove" or "insert" problems in Java usually mean rearranging elements within the same array and returning a new logical length, not actually resizing anything.

Accessing elements — O(1), zero-indexed:

java
int first = nums[0];
nums[2] = 10;          // overwrite index 2
int len = nums.length; // NOTE: .length is a field, not a method (no parentheses)

Traversal

java
for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(nums[i]);
}
// or enhanced for-loop (when you don't need the index)
for (int n : nums) {
    System.out.println(n);
}

Why insertion/deletion is O(n) in Java specifically
Since the array can't resize, "inserting" at index i in a conceptual sense means shifting every element from i onward one position to the right — and you need a big-enough backing array to already have room. This is exactly why ArrayList exists (it manages this resizing for you internally), but for these interview-style problems, you work directly with the fixed-size int[] and shift manually.
